1. WebAssembly components (separate files, built from source we publish)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The WASM files are separate artifacts that the app loads at run time. Their
sources, build recipes and the pinned toolchains are in this repository, and CI
publishes each build as a GitHub release (`occt-<hash>`, `planegcs-<hash>`) so
that anyone can rebuild or replace them. OpenSCAD's WASM is OpenSCAD's own
build, mirrored the same way (`openscad-<hash>`).

  OpenCascade Technology (OCCT) 8.0.1 (commit b8f597c677811d1f9f4d8a97f5ae2825c0353a42)
    The geometry kernel, in extrudo_occt_single.wasm.
    License: GNU LGPL 2.1 with the Open CASCADE exception.
    https://dev.opencascade.org/ and https://github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT
    Built with the libcascade toolchain (below) and Emscripten 6.0.5. The
    toolchain's pinned inputs (including FreeType and RapidJSON, which OCCT can
    use) are recorded in the build's provenance file.

  Extrudo's C++ facade over OCCT (packages/kernel/occt/facade/)
    Compiled into the same WASM file. License: LGPL-2.1-or-later
    (packages/kernel/occt/facade/LICENSE).

  libcascade / @libcascade/toolchain 3.0.2 (the build toolchain, taucad/opencascade.js)
    https://github.com/taucad/opencascade.js
    Toolchain: MIT. The OCCT it builds: see above.
    Known issue we work around: taucad/opencascade.js#40 (open as of 2026-10-02):
    calling delete() on a JavaScript handle often does not free the memory the
    C++ object owns (NCollection_Array1, BRepAlgoAPI_Cut without Clear(),
    BRepFilletAPI_MakeFillet). It is why our C++ facade owns every OCCT object and
    JavaScript only holds integer handles. https://github.com/taucad/opencascade.js/issues/40

  manifold-3d 3.5.4 (the mesh kernel mesh bodies live in), in manifold.wasm
    https://github.com/elalish/manifold
    License: Apache-2.0. A separate WASM file, loaded only by a design that
    imports a mesh file (STL, 3MF, OBJ: ADR-0066 §3), from the npm package's
    own build. It runs in the kernel worker and in Node.

  QuickJS, in emscripten-module.wasm
    The JavaScript engine a Script feature's code runs in, compiled to
    WebAssembly by the quickjs-emscripten project (0.31.0): the
    `@jitl/quickjs-wasmfile-release-sync` variant's own build, driven through
    `quickjs-emscripten-core`. License: MIT.
    https://bellard.org/quickjs/ and https://github.com/justjake/quickjs-emscripten
    A separate WASM file, loaded by the kernel worker and by Node only for a
    design that has a script (P5-02, ADR-0070 §2); the app loads it through the
    same `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` allowance as the other two.

  planegcs (the sketch constraint solver), in planegcs.wasm
    FreeCAD's PlaneGCS, via https://github.com/Salusoft89/planegcs
    License: LGPL-2.1-or-later. We build it ourselves with a small patch
    (packages/sketch/planegcs/planegcs.patch, LGPL-2.1-or-later). The
    JavaScript wrapper comes from the @salusoft89/planegcs npm package 1.2.0
    (LGPL-2.0-or-later). The WASM contains code from Eigen (MPL-2.0,
    https://eigen.tuxfamily.org/) and Boost (Boost Software License 1.0,
    https://www.boost.org/), both used header-only at build time.

  OpenSCAD 2026.10.05 (a nightly snapshot), in openscad.wasm and openscad.js
    https://openscad.org/ and https://github.com/openscad/openscad
    License: GPL-2.0-or-later. OpenSCAD's own WebAssembly build
    (https://files.openscad.org/snapshots/OpenSCAD-2026.10.05-WebAssembly-web.zip,
    sha256 a27c885251865aca407af68908ced454266f902cd2b59e1923e33a97c825ef3d),
    mirrored as the release openscad-<hash> of this repository with one change:
    its JavaScript glue reads the heap limit from a module option
    (packages/openscad/openscad.mjs). Loaded only by a design that imports a
    .scad file (ADR-0071), in a worker of its own. The WASM also contains, as
    built by OpenSCAD's own WebAssembly toolchain:
      CGAL (GPL-3.0-or-later and LGPL-3.0-or-later, https://www.cgal.org/)
      Manifold (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/elalish/manifold)
      GMP and MPFR (LGPL-3.0-or-later, https://gmplib.org/, https://www.mpfr.org/)
      Boost (Boost Software License 1.0) and Eigen (MPL-2.0)
      Clipper2 (Boost Software License 1.0, https://github.com/AngusJohnson/Clipper2)
      lib3mf (BSD-2-Clause, https://github.com/3MFConsortium/lib3mf)
      libzip (BSD-3-Clause) and zlib (Zlib)
      FreeType (FreeType License), HarfBuzz (MIT), Fontconfig (MIT-style)
      GLib (LGPL-2.1-or-later), libxml2 (MIT), cairo (LGPL-2.1 or MPL-1.1)
      nlohmann/json (MIT), oneTBB (Apache-2.0), double-conversion (BSD-3-Clause)
    CGAL's GPL-3.0-or-later parts make the combined WASM a GPL-3.0-or-later work,
    which Extrudo's own license is.

How to get the sources of the LGPL parts
  - OCCT: https://github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT at the commit above, or the
    build inputs in packages/kernel/occt/ (libcascade.config.ts, facade/) with
    `pnpm occt build` (Docker, about 15 minutes), documented in
    packages/kernel/occt/README.md.
  - planegcs: packages/sketch/planegcs/ (build.sh pins the upstream commit,
    planegcs.patch is our change) with `pnpm planegcs build`, documented in
    packages/sketch/planegcs/README.md.
  - OpenSCAD (GPL): the snapshot's URL and checksum above, and OpenSCAD's
    sources at https://github.com/openscad/openscad (the snapshot is the
    master branch of that date); `pnpm openscad build` downloads it again.
  - The released builds: the GitHub releases named occt-<hash>,
    planegcs-<hash> and openscad-<hash> of this repository. The hash in use is written to
    dist/.inputs-hash next to the files.
  You may replace either WASM file with your own build of the same inputs.


